# prompt.history — meaningful prompts and design decisions

## 1. [2026-04-12] Auth expiry detection + --reauth for --update

User ran `--update --verbose` on 262 playlist folders and hit auth expired errors on every single folder — no fail-fast.

Prompts:
- "does this mean notebooklm auth expires?" — yes, Google session cookies expire
- "I want to implement this notebooklm status, to prevent all auth failure in case the auto expires, btw, give me a --reauth that copy the auth data again and overwrite the current one"
- "did you upgrade the code?" — realized script is a symlink, changes are live
- "but I didn't get the new update benefit, still error all the way" — pre-flight said OK but subprocesses still failed

Key discoveries & fixes:
1. `notebooklm status` is offline-only (reads context.json cache), never hits network — useless for auth validation
2. `notebooklm list` hits network but returns exit code 0 on auth failure — error is in JSON body `"error": true`
3. Pre-flight was testing global `~/.notebooklm/` auth, but subprocesses use local `.notebooklm/` copies (stale)
4. `notebooklm auth` was renamed to `notebooklm login` in newer CLI versions

Final design:
- `check_auth_valid()` uses `notebooklm list --json` and checks both exit code and JSON `"error"` field
- `run_update()` pre-flight tests with a real local folder's `.notebooklm/` auth (not global)
- If local is stale but global is valid → auto-runs `run_reauth()` to refresh all copies (no manual `--reauth` needed)
- If both expired → fail fast with "run notebooklm login"
- `--reauth` flag available as standalone for manual refresh

## 2. [2026-04-15] Fix --update summary: only list genuine new uploads

User ran `--update` and got a summary where 4 "updated" folders listed far more "+ [###] title" entries than videos actually added. For MIT microeconomics, the summary showed 49 lines but only 1 new video was actually inserted.

Prompts:
- "what happened to that 4 updates?" — confused by inflated title lists under each updated folder
- "if only 1 video inserted to the playlist, everyone's name is re-indexed/re-numbered, but I don't care the new numbering for existing video, I just want to know the newly added/inserted video and what is the playlist or folder name."

Root cause:
- `run_update()` extracted new-upload titles with regex `→ (\[\d+\] .+)`, which also captured reindex output
- Reindex emits `→ [###] title` three times per run: (1) dry-run plan listing, (2) execution `... OK` lines, (3) text-back-to-video-effort renames
- So a single new insert that triggers renumbering of N existing sources produced ~2N+1 "+ ..." lines in summary

Fix:
- Tightened regex to `^ {8}→ (\[\d+\] .+)$` with re.MULTILINE
- Genuine YouTube upload rename (line ~3276) uses 8-space indent with arrow at start of line
- Reindex lines all use 2-space indent with `old_label → ...` (text before arrow) → now excluded
- Whisper detection unchanged (distinct `Adding transcript as text source:` marker)

## 3. [2026-04-17] Fix SSH port 22 for genesis + --notebook-url takeover of manually-imported notebooks

Two unrelated issues bundled into one session.

### 3a. SSH port 22 fix
User noticed `--remote-sshfs` whisper fallback was failing with `Connection refused` on port 22922 even though genesis listens on 22.

Prompt:
- "still using port 22922?" — pointing at repeated SSH upload failures in the trace

Root cause:
- User had previously edited local `/etc/services` to rename ssh → 22922 for security
- That changes what `ssh`/`scp`/`sshfs` resolve to when no `-p`/`-P`/`-o port=` is given
- Genesis itself still listens on 22, so every default-port outbound call was dead on arrival

Fix:
- Added explicit port flags to all 9 ssh/scp/sshfs invocations in `whisper_via_sshfs()`, `whisper_via_ssh()`, and `mount_sshfs()`:
  - `ssh -p 22 …`
  - `scp -P 22 …`
  - `sshfs … -o port=22,reconnect,ServerAliveInterval=15`

### 3b. Take over manually-imported notebooks with --notebook-url / --setup
User often creates notebooks via YouTube's "Import to NotebookLM" button, then wants the script to take over (rename to `[001]` format, whisper-fill failed videos, etc.). Previously the script could only find-or-create by title, which misses manually-created notebooks with non-standard titles.

Prompts:
- "on --setup (meaning it is not finished), could I manually change which notebook url of a folder pointing to? ... 我只是手動創建 notebook by youtube's 'import into notebooklm', then I want to make you take over the rest manipulation."
- "I prefer a 3rd solution, since I've created that notebook, the easiest way is it's notebook url. could I give you a notebook url, and you know what rest to take over?"
- "how about --setup url (url of a youtube playlist or url of a notebook?)"
- "but if I already in pwd of that implicit folder/notebook, it's index.list has the information (playlist url)"
- "case 3 may be pwd (./index.list) or a path as 2nd argument (notebookurl, path; notebookurl path; path, notebookurl; path notebookurl) this way, not pwd, but path is the folder having path/index.list for us."
- "and with 2 arguments, space or comma must be toleranced."
- "and the bind manually case, usually the folder name, the notebook name are not standardized, we need to perform the rename (Joshua_, no space,etc)"
- "--notebook-url only accept one argument notebook-url? or can take 2nd path of the book folder? and no order (who is in front)"

Design:
- `extract_notebook_id(url_or_uuid)` — parses `https://notebooklm.google.com/notebook/<uuid>[/...]` or a bare UUID
- `classify_setup_arg(arg)` — returns `('notebook', uuid) | ('playlist', url) | ('path', resolved) | ('unknown', arg)`
- `--notebook-url` flag: `nargs='+'`, accepts 1-2 values (URL + optional path, any order, space or comma separated)
- Positional args for `--setup`/`--auto` use the same classification
- Both pools are merged and each token is split on commas, then classified and deduplicated
- `bind_existing_folder(nb_id)` — sets up `.notebooklm/` auth + `notebooklm use <id>` in current cwd
- `standardize_folder_and_notebook(playlist_title)` — renames cwd to `sanitize_folder_name(title)` (os.rename + chdir) and renames bound notebook to `Joshua_<title>` via `notebooklm rename`; no-ops when already standard; skipped if target path exists
- Called automatically whenever `notebook_url` is bound (both classic `auto_setup` with `--notebook-url`, and the bind-existing-folder path)

Supported invocation forms (all equivalent, order-agnostic):
```
--setup <playlist_url> <notebook_url>
--setup <notebook_url> <playlist_url>
--setup "<notebook_url>,<playlist_url>"
--setup <notebook_url> <path>           # uses <path>/index.list
--setup <notebook_url>                  # uses ./index.list
--notebook-url <nb_url>                 # standalone: rebind cwd and exit
--notebook-url <nb_url> <path>
--notebook-url "<nb_url>,<path>"
```

Error cases:
- Bare path alone → "can't know which notebook"
- Notebook URL alone with no `index.list` (and no path) → error asking for playlist URL
- Multiple different notebooks / playlists / paths → error
- Invalid `--notebook-url` value → error

## 4. [2026-04-17] index.list v2: notebook url + title headers, manual-edit priority, folder follows title

Extends the bind-takeover feature from #3 with a persistent, user-editable record in `index.list` itself, plus detection of already-healthy sources so bind doesn't redo completed work.

### 4a. Detect already-imported healthy sources on bind
Prompt:
- "can you detect that newly connected notebook might have ok and healthy source already imported, and no need to do anything or transcription even the add_source_ok.txt doesn't know about the real ok list?"

Problem: after a manual YouTube "Import to NotebookLM", the notebook already contains most/all sources, but `add_source_ok.txt` / `add_source_video2txt.txt` are empty, so `-r` would re-add everything.

Fix:
- New `sync_tracking_from_notebook()` helper extracts the match+write half of `reindex_sources()` (no rename, no prompt)
- Called automatically at the end of the bind-existing path
- Cleans error sources first, matches remaining via `_match_sources_by_title` (URL for native YouTube, `#URL` header for whisper text), writes both tracking files
- Reports `Recorded N healthy sources (X native, Y whisper text); M missing`

### 4b. Bind hint mentions --update
Prompt:
- "when --setup notebookurl path, I saw you hint that with --resume -r to go on, add --update (also work) for the hint"

Fix: rewrote the post-bind hint to list `-r`, `--update` (from parent dir), and `--reindex` as the three ways to continue.

### 4c. New index.list spec — lines 2 & 3
Prompts:
- "I want to change the spec. in index.list, we add 2nd and 3rd line (align well) ... and this is the default. but sometimes, I will manually edit this file, especially notebook title ... this manual change of index.list notebook title part must be preserved, never lost, and take the highest priority, especially on notebook title naming. Is that possible? any logic problem?"
- Clarification: "on the first bind, the Joshua_ naming always win, unless there is 3rd line at index.list and that title is something different from the default title (I changed it on purpose). So A, changed 3rd line title always win, B, Joshua_ the default standardized ... 3, is the imported title of the notebook (mostly need to be standardized)"
- "don't worry about the web UI renaming, it should become back to standardized or the 3rd changed on purpose"
- "also, the folder name should honor 3rd line renaming. once changed and different from standardized, the folder name follows. But the folder name must not have the Joshua_ part at all."
- "and for old index.list or first time creation or bind, where did you get 2nd line, the notebook url? some old spec index.list don't have 2nd and 3rd line. append it."
- "with a --setup notebook-url or --notebook-url url binding, will you overwrite 2nd line url?" — yes, unconditional rebind
- "the 2nd line need one more tab" — tab alignment fix

New file format:
```
# playlist:		<playlist_url>
# notebook url:		<notebook_url>
# notebook title:	<notebook_title>
1	<video_url>	"<title>"	<YYYYMMDD>
...
```
Tabs: 2 tabs after `playlist:` and `notebook url:`, 1 tab after `notebook title:` — aligns values at col 24 (tab stop 3, 8-char tabs).

Priority order for notebook title:
1. Line 3 (user-edited) always wins
2. `Joshua_<playlist_title>` default
3. Cloud / web-UI title — overwritten to match the winner

Folder name = `derive_folder_name(line 3)` = strip `Joshua_` prefix + `sanitize_folder_name`. Folder never carries the `Joshua_` prefix (notebook title does). Folder rename follows line 3 edits on the next `--setup` / `--auto` / `--update` pass.

### 4d. Preservation + backfill
- `write_index_list(..., notebook_url=None, notebook_title=None)` — when either param is `None`, the existing value in the file is preserved; never clobbered.
- `update_index_metadata()` — header-only rewrite that keeps the video body intact.
- `standardize_folder_and_notebook()` seeds missing lines 2 (from current binding) and 3 (`Joshua_<playlist_title>`).
- `backfill_index_metadata()` — lightweight, called after notebook-selected check in every mode including `-r` / `--reindex`; seeds missing lines 2 (from current binding) and 3 (from cloud title, safe preservation). No renames, no `yt-dlp` call — minimal disruption.

### 4e. --update in-place mode
Problem: custom folder names (from line 3 edits) broke `run_update`, which launched subprocesses with `cwd=folder_path.parent` + `--auto <url>`, making `auto_setup` create a fresh `sanitize_folder_name(title)` subfolder alongside the renamed one.

Fix:
- `run_update` now uses `cwd=str(folder_path)` (subprocess starts inside the folder itself)
- `auto_setup` detects in-place mode — if cwd already has an `index.list` whose playlist ID matches `url`, skip folder creation/chdir entirely
- Custom-named folders survive indefinite `--update` batch runs

### 4f. Code map
| Function | Role |
|---|---|
| `read_index_metadata(idx)` | Parse `# playlist` / `# notebook url` / `# notebook title` headers |
| `write_index_list(..., nb_url, nb_title)` | Full rewrite; preserves metadata when params are `None` |
| `update_index_metadata(idx, …)` | Header-only rewrite |
| `derive_folder_name(title)` | Strip `Joshua_` + sanitize → folder name |
| `build_notebook_url(nb_id)` | UUID → full NotebookLM URL |
| `standardize_folder_and_notebook(title, idx)` | Enforce line 3 on cloud notebook + folder rename |
| `backfill_index_metadata(idx)` | Lightweight seed for legacy files (no rename) |
| `sync_tracking_from_notebook(...)` | Record already-imported sources into tracking files |

Spec also written to `INDEX_LIST_SPEC.md` (10 sections, on-disk reference).

## 5. [2026-04-17] Global rename: bookmarklm → notebooklm (historical typo fix)

User flagged that the project name had been `bookmarklm` throughout the codebase — a typo-mashup of "bookmark" + "NotebookLM". The project manipulates NotebookLM, not bookmarks.

Prompt:
- "a critical error on naming, the folder, the main script and alias (either ~/.zshrc or ~/.local/bin/* that our main project/script name should be notebooklm not bookmarklm, did you see that?"

Two-phase rename (phase B run outside the session by the user since cwd needed to move):

### Phase A — in-session (commit `a4390b7`)
- `sed` replace 59 occurrences of `bookmarklm` → `notebooklm` across 5 files (`manipulate_bookmarklm_from_yt_playlist.py`, `CLAUDE.md`, `USING_COLAB_4WHISPER.md`, `command.md`, `command_last_working_ok`)
- `git mv` the main script: `manipulate_bookmarklm_from_yt_playlist.py` → `manipulate_notebooklm_from_yt_playlist.py`
- Replace `~/.local/bin/manipulate_bookmarklm_from_yt_playlist` symlink with new-name symlink pointing at the renamed script (still-old folder path for the moment)

### Phase B — `/tmp/finish_rename.sh` run outside session
- `mv` the local folder: `~/work/manipulate_bookmarklm_from_yt_playlist` → `~/work/manipulate_notebooklm_from_yt_playlist`
- Repoint symlink to the new absolute path
- Move Claude auto-memory dir (`~/.claude/projects/-Users-joshua-work-manipulate-*-from-yt-playlist`)
- `gh repo rename manipulate_notebooklm_from_yt_playlist` (updates `origin` URL; GitHub keeps a redirect)

Verification:
- cwd, symlink, git remote, memory dir all reflect the new name
- `MEMORY.md` + existing feedback memories survived the move

## 6. [2026-04-17] Two new "3-ai" skills: dogs-debate (adversarial) + owls-council (collaborative review)

User wanted reusable patterns for orchestrating multi-AI conversations via local CLI tools. Two complementary skills created in `~/.claude/skills/`:

### 6a. `3-ai-dogs-debate`
**Claude drives, Gemini CLI + Codex CLI argue.** For stress-testing falsifiable claims adversarially.

Prompts:
- "from now on, can you run gemini (cli) and codex (cli) AI tools and interact with those 2 and have a wonderful discussion?"
- "3, is interesting, and remember to note down everything in the progress for later reference. let's do 20 rounds"
- "summarize your methodology, how do you arrange this 3 dogs fight on a kickstart topic, how to manage that, and how to form a /skills for further reuse?"
- "the name should be 3-ai-dogs-debate"
- "Codex needs --skip-git-repo-check if cwd directory isn't a git repo. modify our skill."

Six-move methodology captured in `SKILL.md`:
1. Concrete falsifiable seed claim with inlined rules of engagement
2. Role + constraint priming (one-sentence persona + hard word cap)
3. Full transcript feed-forward each round (coherence across 20+ rounds)
4. Driver script, not manual orchestration (`driver_template.py`)
5. Codex output stripping (critical — extract between first `codex\n` and `tokens used`)
6. Synthesis pass after N rounds (Gemini, cheap) → agreements / disagreements / strongest args / next experiments

Config knobs: rounds (3–30 guided), `reasoning_effort=medium` (halves Codex latency, no quality loss), word cap (default 250), default log `./3-ai-dogs-debate.md` in cwd, `codex exec --skip-git-repo-check` so non-git cwd works.

Live test on 2026-04-17: ran 20 rounds on "fading PDH/PDL wicks has positive expectancy on intraday ES/NQ tape" — ~11 min wall time, converged with 3 concrete follow-up experiments.

### 6b. `3-ai-owls-council`
**Claude drafts a proposal; Gemini = Chief Architect, Codex = Senior Implementation Reviewer.** For pre-screening plans/diffs/designs before asking the human for approval. Companion to dogs-debate (dogs fight, owls counsel).

Prompts:
- "I want another skill, that this time, 3 ai (claude, gemini, codex) no more debate, but they cowork on a vibe coding small medium session."
- "I'd prefer max of 10 rounds, for the 3 owls"

Flow:
- Round 1 silent (each owl reviews independently, no cross-exposure)
- Rounds 2+ rebuttal (each owl sees the other's priors)
- Fixed output scaffold: `Blockers / Concerns / Nits / Suggestions / Verdict`
- Convergence check every round (Gemini one-liner "agree on all material blockers? y/n") — 2× `yes` in a row → early stop
- Hard cap 10, typical 2–3
- Final synthesis: ranked action list `Must-fix / Should-fix / Consider / Out-of-scope` with `[⚠ disputed]` tags on contested items

Role palette (swap by proposal type): Architect / Security Auditor / Performance Engineer / UX Reviewer / API Designer / Data Modeler / QA Engineer / Test Author / Refactoring Critic / Operational Readiness.

### 6c. Live change detection confirmation
Via claude-code-guide agent: `~/.claude/skills/` changes take effect in running sessions without restart (per [Claude Code docs — Live change detection](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills.md#live-change-detection)). Only caveat: a brand-new top-level skills directory requires a restart, but `~/.claude/skills/` pre-existed so new skills are picked up immediately.

## 7. [2026-04-17] --add-video + fractional reordering + 5-column index.list schema

User wanted to grow a notebook over time with extra YouTube videos that aren't from the source playlist, and to reorder any row freely via manual edit.

Prompts:
- "I have a major new feature to add --add-video https://single-youtube-video either takes one 2nd argument (a folder path order;comma space separator doesn't matter) or missing 2nd (current cwd) that will add this video to a notebook (folder index.list)"
- "yes, Does not re-sort by date. Does not touch the playlist URL on line 1, no need for a --re-add flag but I do have one issue here"
- "it's very likely I keep growing this add-on single video. the first time you append it to the tail is fine. What if later I want to place it at the top. that is not --add-video, but kind of re-sort effort. Do I simply edit the desired order at index.list file? how do I tell you?"
- "yes, apparently we need both, and especially the regular --update will need this. plus when I move the entry to the top, I will give number like 0000.5 0.51 meaning it is before 0001, is that ok for you?"
- "能讓第四欄盡量對整齊嗎？ title 取一個稍微大的寬值. 讓第四欄位勁量整齊"
- "再者, 讓第四欄變成第五欄, 而手動額外新增的 video 需要第四欄 \"extra\" 表示這是非原本 playlist 內的, 是我手動加入甚至排序來的."

### 7a. `--add-video <URL> [<path>]`
- Takes one YouTube video URL; optional folder path (order-agnostic, comma-tolerant; default = cwd)
- Requires the target folder to have `index.list` + `.notebooklm/` binding
- Skip silently if URL already in `add_source_ok.txt` / `add_source_video2txt.txt` / `index.list`
- Compute next sequential integer (`math.floor(max_existing) + 1`)
- Upload via existing pipeline (native YouTube → whisper fallback chain honoring `--colab-url`, `--remote-sshfs`, `--no-local-fallback`, etc.)
- Append one row to `index.list` with `source="extra"` marker, no sort_date

New function `add_single_video()` placed between `_match_sources_by_title` and `sync_tracking_from_notebook` to keep upload primitives co-located. Dispatcher inserted in `main()` before setup_mode, exits 0/1 after completion.

### 7b. Fractional index reordering
To reorder any row, user edits column 1 to any float (`0.5` for "before 1", `-1` for "always first", `0000.5` zero-padded parses the same as `0.5`), saves, and runs `--reindex` (or lets `--update` sweep pick it up).

`reindex_sources()` now:
1. Parses column 1 as `float`, not `int` (legacy ints still work)
2. Sorts entries by the float index (stable on ties, file order tiebreaks)
3. Renumbers to clean sequential integers 1..N — fractions consumed
4. Rewrites `index.list` preserving headers 1–3 and per-row `source` + `sort_date` columns
5. Proceeds with the existing match-and-rename flow on notebook sources

`_match_sources_by_title()` gained **Strategy -1 URL equality**: match notebook source's native `url` field against `index.list` row URLs. This is the most direct match possible and crucially handles `--add-video` entries (whose URLs aren't in the source playlist but still equal the source.url stored by NotebookLM).

### 7c. New 5-column data-row schema with `source` marker
Columns are now `idx | url | title | source | sort_date`. The `source` column is empty for playlist-native rows and `extra` for `--add-video` rows; reserved `extra:<tag>` namespace for future markers.

Visual alignment constants:
- `INDEX_TITLE_WIDTH = 100` — title (with quotes) pads to this width so downstream columns align
- `INDEX_SOURCE_WIDTH = 7` — source marker pads to this width

New helpers `parse_index_entry_line()` + `format_index_entry_line()` — single point of truth for schema. Three legacy parser sites (at the main pipeline resume-load, at reindex input, and at update-list discovery) all now route through `parts[2].rstrip().strip('"')` to tolerate the width padding.

**Backward compatibility:** legacy 4-column files (where column 4 was `sort_date` before the `source` column existed) are parsed correctly — if `parts[3]` matches `t:\d+` or exactly 8 digits, it's treated as `sort_date` with empty `source`. Next `--reindex`/`--update` rewrites into the new 5-column format and the ambiguity is gone.

`INDEX_LIST_SPEC.md` updated with: full data-row schema table, fractional-reordering section, legacy tolerance note. `CLAUDE.md` gained three `--add-video` example invocations.

### 7d. `global DEBUG, AUTO_YES` hoisted to top of `main()`
Adding the `--add-video` branch earlier in `main()` exposed a `SyntaxError: name 'DEBUG' is assigned to before global declaration` because the previous code's `global DEBUG, AUTO_YES` was buried inside a later branch. Moved the declaration to the top of `main()`; removed the redundant inner declaration.

## 8. [2026-04-18] Owls council code review of 3726bf7 + parallel-agent fixes

First real use of both new skills on project code. Ran `3-ai-owls-council` on the just-pushed `--add-video` / fractional-reindex / 5-col-schema commit, then used `superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents` to fix the findings.

Prompts:
- "do you know the 3-ai-owls?"
- "run the owls to review, quality control, improve (no extra feature yet) on our current code"
- "yes, use /superpowers whatever to bang the parallel show"

### 8a. Owls council result
Fed them the full diff of `manipulate_notebooklm_from_yt_playlist.py` from commit `3726bf7` (~24 KB / 543 lines). 3 rounds of rebuttal with no early-stop (real disagreements). Verdict: **BLOCK**. 7 blockers, 3 should-fix, 5 consider. Full log saved to `3-ai-owls-council.md` in repo root.

Surprising quality: the owls caught genuine production bugs that would have bitten within days — not just style preferences. Most impactful finds:
- **Silent data loss in `reindex_sources`**: malformed lines dropped + rewrite = permanent loss.
- **`needs_rewrite` always true**: `str(1.0) != "1"` made every `--reindex` rewrite the file unconditionally.
- **Non-atomic `--add-video`**: tracking file written before `index.list`, creating ghost entries on partial failure.

One disputed blocker (index-collision edge case) — I agreed with Codex against Gemini and skipped it.

### 8b. Parallel-agent dispatch
Grouped the 4 highest-priority issues into 3 independent function domains:
- **Agent 1** — `reindex_sources()`: Bug A (data-loss orphan tracking), Bug B (rewrite-detection comparison), Bug C (duplicate-URL handling).
- **Agent 2** — `add_single_video()`: Bug #4 (reorder writes so `index.list` append precedes tracking-file record, both native and whisper paths).
- **Agent 3** — `main()` resume-load + `sync_tracking_from_notebook()`: consolidate onto `parse_index_entry_line()` single source of truth.

All three ran concurrently against the same file. One minor race: Agent 1's first edit was rejected because Agent 2 had shifted line numbers mid-dispatch; Agent 1 re-read and re-applied successfully. No data loss — Edit's file-modified guard worked correctly.

### 8c. Integrated result
`+110 / -41` lines in one file. Syntax passes; parser roundtrip still works across legacy 4-col, new 5-col, short 3-col, and fractional rows. All 4 blockers + 1 should-fix landed as designed.

### 8d. Deferred (not worth one-line churn)
- TSV injection from tabs/newlines in titles — low real-world probability
- Race on concurrent `--add-video` — would need `add_source_working.lock` plumbing reuse
- Disputed index-collision edge case
- "Consider" items: CJK padding widths, URL normalization, magic-number naming, help-text mismatch, non-idiomatic `import math` placement

### 8e. Skills methodology validation
This session was a fair test of whether the two new skills pull their weight:
- **owls-council** surfaced bugs I hadn't seen (even after writing the code myself) — genuinely useful.
- **dispatching-parallel-agents** turned a 4-bug queue into 3 concurrent edits with zero conflicts — noticeably faster than sequential.
Both skills stay in the rotation.

## 9. [2026-04-18] Second parallel-agent sweep: quote preservation, rename warnings, CJK-aware padding

Continued from #8 — tackled the should-fix + top consider items the owls flagged.

Prompts:
- "let's move on the issues"
- "yes,"

Three parallel general-purpose agents, each scoped to one independent change:

### 9a. Quote preservation in `parse_index_entry_line`
Old: `parts[2].rstrip().strip('"')` removed ALL leading/trailing quotes (greedy). Broke titles like `"The "ONE CANDLE" Scalping"` (a real YouTube title pattern).

New: strip exactly ONE wrapping pair only if both ends are `"`. Inner quotes are preserved verbatim.

Verified: `"a"b"` now parses to `a"b` (not `a`). Round-trip through write + parse is lossless.

### 9b. Surfaced `rename_source` failures in `add_single_video`
Old: `rename_source(source_id, indexed)` result ignored — silent failure leaves the notebook showing an auto-fetched title instead of `[NNN] xxx`, user only notices it in the NotebookLM UI.

New: captured return value; on failure emits `⚠ rename_source failed for {id} — title will be reconciled on next --reindex` to stderr. Doesn't abort (source is in, reindex fixes it). Audit confirmed no other silent-bool returns in `add_single_video`.

### 9c. CJK-aware visual-width padding
Old: `f'{title_field:<{INDEX_TITLE_WIDTH}}'` counts Python characters, not display columns. CJK chars take 2 columns but counted as 1, so the `sort_date` column failed to align when CJK-heavy titles mixed with ASCII.

New: two module-level helpers `_visual_width(s)` and `_pad_to_visual_width(s, width)` using `unicodedata.east_asian_width()` to count `W` / `F` chars as 2 columns. `format_index_entry_line` now uses `_pad_to_visual_width`. Added `import unicodedata` at module top.

Verified:
- `'hello world'` (11 cols) + padding to 100 → 89 trailing spaces, total 100 cols.
- `'你好世界'` (8 cols) + padding to 100 → 92 trailing spaces, total 100 cols.
- `'hello 世界'` (10 cols) + padding to 100 → 90 trailing spaces, total 100 cols.

All three rows now have their `sort_date` column starting at the exact same terminal column regardless of CJK content.

